arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: the A72 cluster's OPP table

The last thing mediatek-cpufreq wants, and the one that decides what the
cluster is allowed to do.

Source: the vendor's own table for cluster B, transcribed in
04-docs/A72-OPP-TABLE-2026-08-24.md from ubports-3.18 mt_cpufreq.c. Its
frequencies are kHz and its voltages are steps of 10 uV despite the "10MV"
comment in that file (118000 x 10 uV = 1.18 V, the rail the driver brings up),
so opp-hz = vendor x 1000 and opp-microvolt = vendor x 10.

WHICH BIN, and why the answer differs from the A53 tables three screens above.
The vendor picks one of six from two efuse fields, which we cannot read --
mainline mt6797 has no nvmem node. The A53 tables are the SB bin, chosen
because Android on this phone reports LL topping out at 1547 MHz and all
sixteen LL_SB entries match that capture, so this die is SB. This table is
FY1221, the lowest-clocked of the six, which is a deliberate under-claim and
safe in the only direction that matters: checked entry by entry, FY1221 asks
for at least as much voltage as SB1221 at every frequency the two share
(1963 MHz: 1130 vs 1110 mV; 1599: 1050 vs 1040; 1404, 1287, 1053, 936 and 793
equal). It under-clocks a fast die; it cannot over-volt one.

THREE NARROWINGS.

The top is 1989 MHz, not the vendor's 2145. That is where the cluster already
runs and where it was measured (build #99). Raising the ceiling is #59's
decision, with its own risk, and must not ride along on a change about
governing the cluster.

The bottom is 793 MHz. The vendor continues to 650 and 338, but
BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ has never been driven below ~750 MHz from the running side
and the cluster's power-on gate wants ~750, so mcu_b_idvfs refuses anything
lower. An OPP the clock will always refuse is worse than no OPP.

And nothing below 880 mV appears, which is the floor mt6797-a72.c holds on
VPROC2 for as long as the cluster is powered.

MEASURED, all eleven points, build #100 (2026-08-28), userspace governor,
requested -> reported -> VPROC2:

    793 -> 793 -> 880 mV      1404 -> 1404 -> 1000 mV
    936 -> 936 -> 900 mV      1599 -> 1599 -> 1050 mV
   1053 -> 1053 -> 930 mV     1690 -> 1690 -> 1070 mV
   1170 -> 1170 -> 950 mV     1872 -> 1872 -> 1110 mV
   1287 -> 1287 -> 980 mV     1963 -> 1963 -> 1130 mV
                              1989 -> 1989 -> 1140 mV

Every point lands on its table voltage, up and down, and the cluster returns
to MUXSEL[1:0]=1 afterwards.

That also settles a worry this patch used to carry. mediatek-cpufreq derives an
"intermediate voltage" from the OPP at or above the rate of the clock the
cluster parks on -- armpll_div_pll1 at 1092 MHz, so the 1170 MHz entry and
950 mV -- and it looked as though that would floor the four points below it.
It does not: the floor applies DURING a transition, and the last step of
mtk_cpufreq_set_target() scales back down whenever vproc is below inter_vproc.
880 mV at the bottom, as the table asks.

## The capacities, which this table is what breaks

Adding OPP tables to cpu8/cpu9 completes the set: every possible CPU now has a
cpufreq policy, so init_cpu_capacity_callback() finally sees cpus_to_visit
empty and calls topology_normalize_cpu_scale(), which multiplies each
capacity-dmips-mhz by that policy's cpuinfo_max_freq. It has never run with
real frequencies on this machine before, because the A72s had no policy.

Left alone, the dtsi's 608/617/1024 would have become 472/620/1024 -- the
little cluster down-weighted by a quarter as a side effect of a change about
governing the big one. So the same measurement is restated per MHz, here,
where the OPP tables that trigger the renormalisation are:

    raw = wanted / cpuinfo_max_freq_MHz
    LL  608 / 1547 -> 782     L  617 / 2002 -> 613     B  1024 / 1989 -> 1024

and 782x1547 : 613x2002 : 1024x1989 normalises back to 608 : 617 : 1024.
Identical capacities, arrived at through the arithmetic the kernel is about to
do anyway. It is done as a board-level override rather than in the dtsi
because the premise -- that renormalisation runs at all -- is a property of
this board's OPP tables and is false on mt6797-evb and mt6797-x20-dev.

The caveat is in the DTS comment and is real: renormalisation waits for cpu8's
policy, cpu8 is onlined at ~240 s, and until then the scheduler uses 782/613
verbatim -- LL rated above L instead of just below it. Two Cortex-A53 clusters
1.5% apart in truth, so the misplacement is between near-identical clusters
rather than across the big/little boundary, and SD_ASYM_CPUCAPACITY is set
either way. Shortening it means onlining the A72s earlier, not changing the
numbers back.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -2708,6 +2708,122 @@
 			opp-microvolt = <1000000>;
 		};
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * cpu8-9, Cortex-A72 "B" — VPROC2, and the reason this table is not the
+	 * shape of the two above.
+	 *
+	 * Source: the vendor's own table for cluster B, transcribed in
+	 * 04-docs/A72-OPP-TABLE-2026-08-24.md from ubports-3.18
+	 * mt_cpufreq.c. The vendor's frequencies are kHz and its voltages are
+	 * in steps of 10 uV despite the "10MV" comment in that file
+	 * (118000 x 10 uV = 1.18 V, the rail the driver brings up), so
+	 * opp-hz = vendor x 1000 and opp-microvolt = vendor x 10.
+	 *
+	 * WHICH BIN. The vendor picks one of six tables from two efuse fields.
+	 * We cannot read the efuse — mainline mt6797 has no nvmem node and
+	 * /sys/bus/nvmem/devices is empty — so this is FY1221, CPU_LEVEL_0,
+	 * the lowest-clocked of the six. The A53 tables above are the SB bin,
+	 * because Android on this phone reports LL topping out at 1547 MHz and
+	 * all sixteen LL_SB entries match that capture; the same die is
+	 * therefore SB here too, and FY1221 is a deliberate under-claim. It is
+	 * safe in the only direction that matters: checked entry by entry,
+	 * FY1221 asks for at least as much voltage as SB1221 at every
+	 * frequency the two share (1963: 1130 vs 1110; 1599: 1050 vs 1040;
+	 * 1404, 1287, 1053, 936, 793: equal). It under-clocks a fast die; it
+	 * cannot over-volt one. FIXME: read the efuse and pick the real bin
+	 * once an mtk-efuse node exists.
+	 *
+	 * THREE NARROWINGS, all deliberate:
+	 *
+	 * 1. The top is 1989 MHz, not the vendor's 2145. This is where the
+	 *    cluster already runs and where it was measured (build #99,
+	 *    2026-08-27): 0.65% less clock than 2054 for 20 mV less rail.
+	 *    Raising the ceiling is issue #59's decision and has its own risk;
+	 *    it must not ride along on a change about governing the cluster.
+	 *    The three points above 1989 are simply not offered.
+	 *
+	 * 2. The bottom is 793 MHz. The vendor continues to 650 and 338, but
+	 *    the firmware's BIGIDVFSPLLSETFREQ has never been driven below
+	 *    ~750 MHz from the running side and its power-on gate wants ~750,
+	 *    so mcu_b_idvfs refuses anything lower. An OPP the clock will
+	 *    always refuse is worse than no OPP.
+	 *
+	 * 3. No voltage below 880 mV appears, which is simply the bottom of
+	 *    the retained range; mt6797-a72.c holds the rail at that floor for
+	 *    as long as the cluster is powered.
+	 *
+	 * ONE THING WORTH KNOWING, AND ONE THING IT TURNS OUT NOT TO COST.
+	 * mediatek-cpufreq derives an "intermediate voltage" from the OPP at or
+	 * above the rate of the clock the cluster parks on, and that clock is
+	 * armpll_div_pll1 at 1092 MHz, so the ceil is the 1170 MHz entry and
+	 * 950 mV. That is a floor DURING a transition — the rail is raised to
+	 * max(inter, target) before the mux moves — but not after: the last step
+	 * of mtk_cpufreq_set_target() scales back down whenever vproc is below
+	 * inter_vproc. Measured across all eleven points on build #100, the rail
+	 * settles on exactly the table voltage every time, 880 mV at the bottom
+	 * included. The four points below 1170 MHz save voltage as well as
+	 * clock.
+	 */
+	cpu_opp_b: opp-table-b {
+		compatible = "operating-points-v2";
+		opp-shared;
+
+		opp-1989000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1989000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <1140000>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1963000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1963000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <1130000>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1872000000 {
+			opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1872000000>;
+			opp-microvolt = <1110000>;
+		};
+
+		opp-1690000000 {
